What is docker-compose
================================
docker-compose-yml is where everything connects together. This file belongs in root folder alongside dockerfile.

Things to configure in docker-compose.yml:
---------------------------------------------

* What image each service uses
* Environment variables for each service
* Which ports to expose to your host machine
* A shared network so they can talk to each other
* A volume for PostgresSQL so data persists when container restarts

Using environment variables in docker-compose.yml
====================================================

When setting up docker environment and configure variables, there area couple options to use:

* Option A - Create a 'docker' profile: A dedicated 'application-docker.properties' that has Docker specific config.
Clean separation. Very explicit.

*Option B - Use environment variable overrides in docker-compose.yml: Keep your existing profiles and override specific
properties via environment variables in the compose file. Spring Boot lets environment variables override any
property at runtime.

* Option C - Use prod profile with Docker specific env variables: Use 'application-prod.properties' but pass Docker
specific values through Docker Compose environment variables.

Which approach is best?
----------------------------------------------

Option B is the best approach. The key reasoning is "Properties files define the structure and defaults. Environment
variables provide the actual sensitive values at runtime, regardless of environment."

This is called externalization configuration and its a core twelve factor principle. These are best practices for
building production ready applications

How Spring Boot property override works
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Spring Boot has a priority order for configuration. From highest to lowest priority:

1. Environment variables          ← highest, always wins
2. application-{profile}.properties
3. application.properties         ← lowest priority


So if application-dev.properties says: "spring.datasource.url=${DB_URL_DEV}"

And Docker Compose passes in: "DB_URL_DEV=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/mydb"

Spring Boot uses the Docker compose value. If you run locally without Docker, Intellij's run configuration provides the
values instead. Same property file, different values injected depending on environment.

Structure of docker-compose file
============================================

Example:

services:
  db:
    image: postgres:16-alpine
    environment:
      POSTGRES_DB: GymTracker
      POSTGRES_USER: postgres
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: postgres
    ports:
      - "5432:5432"
    volumes:
      - gymtracker_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data
    networks:
      - gymtracker_network

  app:
    build: .
    restart: on-failure
    ports:
      - "8087:8087"
    environment:
      SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE: dev
      DB_URL_DEV: jdbc:postgresql://db:5432/GymTracker
      DB_USERNAME_DEV: postgres
      DB_PASSWORD_DEV: postgres
      JWT_SECRET_DEV: ${JWT_SECRET_DEV}
    depends_on:
      - db
    networks:
      - gymtracker_network

volumes:
  gymtracker_data:

networks:
  gymtracker_network:


volumes
------------------------

A volume is a persistent storage that exists outside the containers lifecycle.

This has to do when container stops or gets deleted- everything inside it disappears. That is the problem with databases
in containers. If your PostgresSql container restarts, you lose all your data.

aVolume solves this by storing data on your host machines filesystem and mounting it into the container. The container
reads and writes to it like a normal folder, but the data lives on your host machine - not inside the container.

                                Host machine disk          Docker container
                                ──────────────────         ─────────────────
                                gymtracker_data    ←─────→ /var/lib/postgresql/data
                                (persists forever)         (PostgreSQL stores data here)

Container deleted -> data survives because it's on your host machine.


depends_on
---------------------------

Declares dependencies between services. Important that if a service is dependent on another, that service must be declared
before it.

'depends_on' only waits for container to start up - not for the service inside it to be ready. PostgresSQL takes a few
seconds to initialize.] after the container starts. Your Spring Boot app might start before PostreSQL is actually
accepting connections, causing a connection error.

The proper fix is a health check - but for now ' depends_on' is fine

We also add 'restart: on-failure' to the app service as this means if the app container fails to start because db isn't
ready yet, docker will restart it.

networks
------------------------------

Docker creates an isolated internal network. Services on the same network can reach each other by service name.
Services on different networks cannot communicate at all - isolation by default. This is why both services must be
on 'gymtracker_network'

'build .' vs 'image:'
-------------------------------

* 'image: postgres:16-alpine' - tells Docker to pull an existing pre built image from Docker hub - PostgresSQL is a well
known image that already exists publicly.
* 'build:' tells Docker to build a new image from your Dockerfile in the current directory - because your Spring Boot
app is custom code, no pre built image exists

Why "${JWT_SECRET_DEV}" is used and value isn't hardcoded
-----------------------------------------------------------

"${JWT_SECRET_DEV}" syntax in docker-compose.yml means Docker Compose reads this value from a '.env' file in the same
directory, or from your shells environment variables.

So the flow is;

.env file
JWT_SECRET_DEV=yourBase64EncodedSecret
        ↓
docker-compose.yml reads it
JWT_SECRET_DEV: ${JWT_SECRET_DEV}
        ↓
Passes it into the container as an environment variable
        ↓
Spring Boot reads it via ${JWT_SECRET_DEV} in application-dev.properties

This is why it is not hardcoded - the .env file gets added to '.gitignore' so it is not committed to Git.
Docker Compose reads it locally, Spring Boot reads it inside the container.


docker-compose for different profiles
==============================================

Running a full Maven build inside Docker on every 'docker-compose up --build' is slow for local development.

The solution most teams use is two separate compose files:

* docker-compose.yml          → base config, shared between environments
* docker-compose.override.yml → local dev overrides, gitignore
* docker-compose.prod.yml     → production specific config

For local development 'docker-compose.override.yml' would use your pre built JAR:

services:
  app:
    build:
      context: .
      dockerfile: Dockerfile.dev


Where Dockerfile.dev is a simple single stage Dockerfile that just copies your pre built JAR - fast for development.

For production 'docker-compose.prod.yml' uses the full multi staged build.


Why docker-compose.override.yml specifically?
---------------------------------------------------------

This is not just a naming convention - its a Docker compose built in feature. when you run 'docker-compose up', Docker
Compose automatically looks for and merges two files in order:

1. docker-compose.yml        → always loaded
2. docker-compose.override.yml → automatically loaded if it exists

Docker finds override automatically and merges it on top of the base file,you don't need to specify anything. The override
files values win when there is a conflict.

This is why its called 'override' - it's Docker Compose native mechanism for layering config

How the merge works:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Base docker-compose.yml:

services:
  app:
    image: gymtracker
    ports:
      - "8087:8087"
    environment:
      SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE: prod


docker-compose.override.yml:

services:
  app:
    build: .
    environment:
      SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE: dev
      DB_URL_DEV: jdbc:postgresql://db:5432/GymTracker


Merged result when running 'docker-compose up':

services:
  app:
    image: gymtracker      # from base
    build: .               # from override - added
    ports:
      - "8087:8087"        # from base
    environment:
      SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE: dev      # override wins
      DB_URL_DEV: jdbc:postgresql://...# from override - added


The workflow:
----------------------------------

Local development:
docker-compose up
→ loads docker-compose.yml + docker-compose.override.yml automatically
→ builds from local Dockerfile
→ uses dev profile and local env vars

Production deployment:
docker-compose -f docker-compose.yml -f docker-compose.prod.yml up
→ loads base + prod file only
→ uses pre-built image from registry
→ uses prod profile and CI/CD injected secrets

Why this pattern is powerful
---------------------------------------

Developer A clones repo
→ creates their own docker-compose.override.yml with their local settings
→ never commits it
→ runs docker-compose up
→ works immediately

Developer B does the same
→ completely different local settings
→ never conflicts with Developer A
→ both work from the same base config

Each developer has their own local override that never touches the repo. The base config is the shared truth everyone
works from

'depend_on' with health checks
===========================================================

Example:
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

services:
  db:
    image: postgres:16-alpine
    environment:
      POSTGRES_DB: GymTracker
      POSTGRES_USER: ${DB_USERNAME_DEV}
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${DB_PASSWORD_DEV}
    ports:
      - "5432:5432"
    volumes:
      - gymtracker_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data
    networks:
      - gymtracker_network
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U postgres"]
      interval: 10s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 5

  app:
    restart: on-failure
    ports:
      - "8087:8087"
    depends_on:
      db:
        condition: service_healthy
    networks:
      - gymtracker_network


Why use 'pg_ready', rather than waiting for container to start?
---------------------------------------------------------------------

When a PostgreSql container starts, it goes through several stages:

Container starts
      ↓
Linux process launches
      ↓
PostgreSQL initialises shared memory
      ↓
PostgreSQL reads configuration files
      ↓
PostgreSQL replays WAL logs (crash recovery)
      ↓
PostgreSQL starts listening on port 5432
      ↓
PostgreSQL ready to accept connections ✅

This process can take anywhere between 3-10 seconds depending on the machine. During that time the container is running
but PostgreSql is not yet accepting conditions.

Without health checks the app container sees:

db container started ✅
→ start app immediately
→ app tries to connect to PostgreSQL
→ PostgreSQL still initialising
→ connection refused
→ Spring Boot fails to start
→ restart: on-failure kicks in
→ retry... maybe works, maybe doesn't

'pg_ready' specifically checks whether PostgreSql is accepting connections - not just whether the container is running.
It's a PostgreSql built in tool that success only when database is genuinely ready.

With health check:

db container started
→ pg_isready checks every 10 seconds
→ fails while PostgreSQL initialising
→ passes when PostgreSQL ready
→ db marked healthy
→ ONLY THEN does app container start
→ guaranteed database connection on first try

This is a race condition - a time dependent bug that sometimes works and sometimes doesn't depending on how fast your
machine is. Race conditions are notorously hard to debug because they are intermittent.

The health check eliminates race condition entirely by making the dependcy explicit and verified